{"abstract":"Question: Which opportunities can merging geographic location data with Work Sampling (WS) data bring for construction management? Purpose: To identify which opportunities adding geographic information to the random observations made (named in this study as geo-located observations) can bring. Research Method: The authors presented the implementation of a novel adaptation of the WS technique, named Location-Based Work Sampling (LBWS), based on the findings from a Case Study. The research process followed four steps: (1) clarifying the categories of the activities; (2) deciding the confidence interval; (3) collecting and extracting data; and (4) analyzing the data. For adding location data to the technique, the authors used the geographic coordinates provided by smartwatches used by the research team connected to two Global Navigations Satellite Systems (GNSS), and the coordinates obtained from photos taken for each observation. Findings: LBWS consists of a visual graphical approach that facilitates sharing information obtained during the WS application, based on adding geo-location information to the random observations. The technique shows the observations made on construction trades and work categories in the foreground and job site spaces in the background. Limitations: The use of geographic coordinates provided by the photos limits the application of this technique to outdoor activities. Implications: Creating a relatively simple visualization of the observations on the job site layout showed that a more comprehensive analysis of the job site activities could be conducted. Value for practitioners: The LBWS technique can provide a better understanding of the ongoing activities' behavior and contribute to the existing Location-Based Management Systems. Keywords: Location-based Management, Visual Management, Work Sampling. Paper type: Case Study.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Perez, Cristina Toca","Salling, Stephanie Therkelsen","Wandahl, Søren"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-757628"],"filters":{"added":"2024-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"location based work sampling","src":["au"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.au.dk/ws/files/388487808/LCJ_P_rez_C._T._Salling_S._T._Wandahl_S._2023_.pdf","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-757628","ids":["au-0c024927-a0b5-4a45-9049-cc19d0d6a713"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-3-C42B7BA068031299A76510B4125E98C6"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.au.dk/ws/files/388487808/LCJ_P_rez_C._T._Salling_S._T._Wandahl_S._2023_.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"au aarhus university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AU","l3nl":"Aarhus University","l3nn":"AU Aarhus University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AU Aarhus University","namevar":["Aarhus University, Technical Sciences, Department of Civil and Architectural Engineering, Department of Civil and Architectural Engineering - Design and Construction"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Cristina Toca","key":"perezpau","last":"Perez","locid":"4ad4f466-ee62-45af-bd3d-3bbadc185fd7","name":"Perez, Cristina Toca","orcid":"0000-0002-4182-1492","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Stephanie Therkelsen","key":"sallingpau","last":"Salling","locid":"d0d5d6a4-75fc-4e33-bc26-93ffb6887c76","name":"Salling, Stephanie Therkelsen","orcid":"0000-0001-7088-6458","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Søren","key":"wandahlpau","last":"Wandahl","locid":"26a61cee-fb66-475b-9505-6e741352b7e6","name":"Wandahl, Søren","orcid":"0000-0001-8708-6035","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"issn":["15551369"],"journal":"Lean Construction Journal","publisher":"Lean Construction Institute","status":"p","title":"Lean Construction Journal","title_org":"Lean Construction","volume":"2023"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85204699439"],"search":{"DAF":["Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["au-0c024927-a0b5-4a45-9049-cc19d0d6a713"],"src":["AU Aarhus University"],"title":"Location-based Work Sampling","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-757628","year":"2023","yearsub":"2023"}
{"abstract":"The Work Sampling (WS) technique, used worldwide to understand how workers spend their time, represents a time-consuming and costly activity. Therefore, several researchers work on different approaches to automate the data collection using sensor-based and vision-based technologies. The challenge of all the sensor-based approaches is that they do not provide the share of time in different work categories. The lack of knowledge on a possible correlation between Direct Work and, e.g., presence, location, or worker movement represents a gap in the current body of knowledge. Thus, this research aims to understand the correlation between Direct Work as the independent predictor variable; and Movement as the dependent response variable. The authors used the data gathered through the application of WS in five case studies on building renovation projects in Denmark. To explain this correlation. The authors selected a combination of four quantitative techniques: (1) curve estimation; (2) linear regression; (3) ANOVA analysis; and (4) t-test. The correlation of the result is discussed considering three assumptions: (1) the structure of the day; (2) global vs. local; and (3) Movement vs. Transporting and Walking. The result shows a significant correlation between Direct Work and Movement with an average R 2 of 0.328. This is considered acceptable predictability taking the socio-technical system aspect of a construction site into account.","author_count":4,"contributors":["Wandahl, Søren","Perez, Cristina Toca","Salling, Stephanie Therkelsen","Neve, Hasse Højgaard"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-129518"],"filters":{"added":"2023-05","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"corresponding_author":["Wandahl, Søren"],"country":["United Kingdom"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","PricewaterhouseCoopers"],"keyword":["Construction","Efficiency","Walking","Work sampling"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"correlation of construction workers movement and direct work rates","src":["au"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.32738/JEPPM-2023-0013","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-129518","identifier":[{"id":"85165630843","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["au-00672fb3-6adb-4f12-85d2-cd916911dc7e"],"keyword":["Construction","efficiency","transporting","walking","work sampling"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-3-F83C40CEE0F2FE93F7D8972C3D3EBB41"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.32738/JEPPM-2023-0013","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.au.dk/ws/files/316022960/ID_566_13_2_125_137.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"au aarhus university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AU","l3nl":"Aarhus University","l3nn":"AU Aarhus University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AU Aarhus University","namevar":["Aarhus University, Technical Sciences, Department of Civil and Architectural Engineering, Department of Civil and Architectural Engineering - Design and Construction"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"gb","key":"pricewaterhousecoopers","loc":["United Kingdom","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"name":"PricewaterhouseCoopers","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Søren","key":"wandahlpau","last":"Wandahl","locid":"26a61cee-fb66-475b-9505-6e741352b7e6","name":"Wandahl, Søren","orcid":"0000-0001-8708-6035","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Cristina Toca","key":"perezpau","last":"Perez","locid":"4ad4f466-ee62-45af-bd3d-3bbadc185fd7","name":"Perez, Cristina Toca","orcid":"0000-0002-4182-1492","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Stephanie Therkelsen","key":"sallingpau","last":"Salling","locid":"d0d5d6a4-75fc-4e33-bc26-93ffb6887c76","name":"Salling, Stephanie Therkelsen","orcid":"0000-0001-7088-6458","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Hasse Højgaard","key":"nevepau","last":"Neve","name":"Neve, Hasse Højgaard","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.32738/jeppm-2023-0013","issn":["22238379"],"issue":"2","journal":"Journal of Engineering, Project, and Production Management","pages":"125-137","publisher":"Walter de Gruyter","status":"p","title":"Journal of Engineering, Project, and Production Management","volume":"13"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85165630843"],"search":{"DAF":["Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["au-00672fb3-6adb-4f12-85d2-cd916911dc7e"],"src":["AU Aarhus University"],"title":"Correlation of Construction Workers’ Movement and Direct Work Rates","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-129518","year":"2023","yearsub":"2023"}
